Recoverability-Conditioned Authority: 

A Causal Framework for Post-Failure Autonomy Regulation in Long-Horizon AI Agents

ouadi Maakoul 

AI : chatGpt, Deepseek, Gemini, Grok XAi 

Abstract

Long-horizon autonomous agents fail mid-trajectory. Existing systems decide whether to recover; they do not systematically condition how much authority is restored after recovery on independently verified recoverability. This proposal develops a formal and empirical framework for post-failure authority regulation. The central causal architecture is F \to D \to U \to S'_r \to R^\star \to \hat{\mathbf R} \to \pi_A \to P^{\text{post}} \to X \to Y_H, with the experimental intervention Z \to \pi_A. The framework treats recoverability as a latent multidimensional functional R^\star_r \in [0,1]^6, estimated by a verifier \hat{\mathbf R}_r = \mathcal{V}(S_f, u, S'_r, E_r), and formalizes authority as a permission-set transition over a complete distributive lattice. The primary identification strategy is a repeated micro-randomized trial in which the authority policy is randomized at each recovery completion, holding recovery mechanism, diagnosis, checkpoint, budget, and verifier fixed. The primary estimand is \text{ATE}_{Y_H} = \mathbb{E}[Y_H \mid do(Z=1)] - \mathbb{E}[Y_H \mid do(Z=0)], where Y_H = \mathbf{1}\{H > 0\} and H is a pre-registered harm severity variable. The proposal integrates recent advances in interventional mediation analysis, doubly robust causal excursion estimation for micro-randomized trials, constrained reinforcement for bounded decision authority, product-lattice models of agent authority attenuation, and recoverability-constrained self-evolution. The central falsifiable claim is that a post-failure authority policy is causally useful if and only if changing it, while holding recovery fixed, changes safety outcomes.

1. Problem Statement

Long-horizon autonomous agents—systems that execute extended sequences of actions with tool calls, external side effects, and persistent state—fail mid-trajectory. A failure event F at step f triggers a diagnosis D_f, a recovery plan U, and a candidate post-recovery state S'_r. The critical decision is not merely whether to recover, but how much authority to restore after recovery. Existing systems treat recovery as a binary: either the agent resumes with its pre-failure permission set, or it halts. Neither policy is recoverability-conditioned. The central experimental philosophy of this proposal is:

\boxed{
\text{A post-failure authority policy is causally useful only if changing it, while holding recovery fixed, changes safety outcomes.}
}

Formally, the design holds recovery fixed (C_R) while randomizing authority (C_A) and measuring outcomes (\Phi_X). This isolates the causal effect of the authority policy itself, not the effect of recovery quality. The scientific question is whether recoverability-conditioned authority regulation—granting permissions as a function of verified post-recovery state—reduces harm relative to fixed-authority baselines, without unacceptable recovery costs.

2. Notation

Let t \in \mathbb{N} index steps. A failure event F occurs at step f; recovery completes at step r > f. The canonical state is S_t \in \mathcal{S}; active invariants are I_t \subseteq \mathcal{I}; diagnosis is D_t \in \mathcal{D}; recovery plan is U \in \mathcal{U}; candidate post-recovery state is S'_r \in \mathcal{S}. Latent recoverability is R^\star_r \in [0,1]^6; estimated recoverability is \hat{\mathbf R}_r \in [0,1]^6; scalar summary is \hat R_r = g(\hat{\mathbf R}_r). Verification evidence is E^R_r \in \mathcal{E}. Authority vector is A_t \in [0,1]^6; permission set is P_t \subseteq \mathcal{P}_0, where \mathcal{P}_0 = \{\text{read}, \text{write}, \text{delete}, \text{network}, \text{financial}, \text{memory}, \text{deployment}, \text{recovery}\}. Criticality is C \in [0,1]; recovery budget is B_R; randomized authority assignment is Z \in \{0,1\}; randomized recovery method is Z_{\text{rec}} \in \{\text{verified}, \text{retry}\}. Harm is H \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}; primary outcome is Y_H = \mathbf{1}\{H > 0\}. The augmented state is \mathcal{X}_t = (S_t, I_t, D_t, R^\star_t, \hat{\mathbf R}_t, E^R_t, A_t, P_t, B_R), with R^\star_t listed for bookkeeping only and not observable by any controller.

3. The Three-Layer Recoverability Model

3.1 Latent Recoverability Functional

Recoverability is not an intrinsic scalar property of a state. It depends on state, available actions, environment, recovery budget, future disturbances, and verification criterion. Define the latent recoverability functional as:

\boxed{
R_r^\star = \mathcal{R}(S_f, D_f, u, B_R, \mathcal{E}) \in [0,1]^6
}

with components:

r^\star_{\text{state}} = P(\text{canonical state restored} \mid S_f, D_f, u, B_R, \mathcal{E}),

r^\star_{\text{invariant}} = P(\text{all required invariants restored} \mid S_f, D_f, u, B_R, \mathcal{E}),

r^\star_{\text{effect}} = P(\text{external effects within permitted bounds} \mid S_f, D_f, u, B_R, \mathcal{E}),

r^\star_{\text{evidence}} = P(\text{evidence sufficient for verification} \mid S_f, D_f, u, B_R, \mathcal{E}),

r^\star_{\text{cost}} = P(\text{recovery cost within budget} \mid S_f, D_f, u, B_R, \mathcal{E}),

r^\star_{\text{reversibility}} = P(\text{recovery remains reversible} \mid S_f, D_f, u, B_R, \mathcal{E}).

These are counterfactual probabilities, latent because they are not directly observable by any controller. The functional \mathcal{R} is pre-specified; it is a recoverability functional, not metaphysical ground truth. Each component is bounded in [0,1]. Monotonicity in budget holds: if B_R^{(1)} \leq B_R^{(2)}, then r^\star_{\text{cost}}(B_R^{(1)}) \geq r^\star_{\text{cost}}(B_R^{(2)}).

3.2 Estimated Recoverability Vector

The verifier \mathcal{V} operates on observables only:

\boxed{
\hat{\mathbf R}_r = \mathcal{V}(S_f, u, S'_r, E_r) \in [0,1]^6
}

with components:

\hat{\mathbf R}_r = (\hat r_{\text{state}}, \hat r_{\text{invariant}}, \hat r_{\text{effect}}, \hat r_{\text{evidence}}, \hat r_{\text{cost}}, \hat r_{\text{reversibility}}).

The vector is the controller input. This permits permission-specific recoverability. For example, if \hat r_{\text{state}} = .95 and \hat r_{\text{effect}} = .25, the controller may authorize \{\text{read}, \text{recovery}\} while denying \{\text{network}, \text{financial}, \text{delete}\}. The scalar summary \hat R_r = g(\hat{\mathbf R}_r) is used only for reporting and calibration.

A critical consistency requirement: the verifier must not depend on the latent R^\star_r. If a measurement-error model is desired, it must be stated separately as \hat{\mathbf R}_r = R^\star_r + \eta_r with \eta_r \sim \text{error distribution}, but this is a measurement model, not a causal input. The operational verifier \mathcal{V}(S_f, u, S'_r, E_r) is grounded in independently verifiable evidence: checkpoint hashes, invariant checks, external-effect logs, sandbox rollback proofs, and verifier signatures.

3.3 Non-Identifiability of R^\star

R_r^\star \neq \hat{\mathbf R}_r \quad \text{in general}.

The controller cannot access R_r^\star. Any authority policy is a function of (\hat{\mathbf R}_r, E^R_r, C, P^{\text{pre}}), not of R_r^\star. This non-identifiability is structural, not a limitation of sample size.

3.4 Calibration

Let R^{\star,\text{bin}}_{r,d} = \mathbf{1}\{R^\star_{r,d} \geq \theta_d\} for pre-registered thresholds \theta_d. The verifier is calibrated per dimension if:

P(R^{\star,\text{bin}}_{r,d} = 1 \mid \hat r_{r,d} = q) = q \quad \forall q \in [0,1].

Expected Calibration Error per dimension is:

\text{ECE}_d = \sum_{m=1}^M \frac{|B_{m,d}|}{n} \left| \text{acc}(B_{m,d}) - \text{conf}(B_{m,d}) \right|.

In a controlled pilot with failure injection at known steps, a ground-truth recoverability oracle can be constructed. Without an oracle, calibration is only relative to a proxy, and the claim must be weakened to \text{ECE}_d(\hat{\mathbf R}, \text{proxy}) \leq \epsilon_{\text{ECE}}.

4. Permission Lattice and Scoped Recovery

4.1 Permission Lattice

Let \mathcal{P}_0 be the primitive permission set. The permission lattice is:

\mathcal{P} = (\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{P}_0), \subseteq)

with \bot = \varnothing, \top = \mathcal{P}_0, meet =\cap, join =\cup. This is a complete, distributive, complemented lattice. The action-permission mapping is:

\Gamma: \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{P}_0),

and the authorized action set is:

\mathcal{A}(P_t) = \{a \in \mathcal{A} : \Gamma(a) \subseteq P_t\}.

Recent work on faceted authority attenuation formalizes agent authority as an element of a product lattice over seven constraint dimensions: scope, spend, depth, time, reputation, values, and reversibility, with delegation as a monotone function on that lattice. This ensures that delegated capabilities can only be attenuated, never amplified. The present framework specializes this to recovery contexts, where the constraint dimension of primary interest is the recoverability of the post-failure state.

4.2 Scoped Recovery Capability

The scoped recovery capability is:

\boxed{
P_{\min}^{\text{rec}}(S_f, D_f, u) = \bigcup_{a \in \text{RecPlan}(S_f, D_f, u)} \Gamma(a)
}

where \text{RecPlan}(S_f, D_f, u) is the finite set of actions in the verified recovery plan u. This is the join of the permission sets required by the recovery plan actions:

P_{\min}^{\text{rec}} = \bigvee_{a \in \text{RecPlan}} \Gamma(a).

This is least-privilege: the controller grants exactly the permissions the recovery plan needs.

4.3 Recovery-Critical Exemption and Deadlock Avoidance

Axiom (Recovery-Critical Exemption):

P_{r+1} \supseteq P_{\min}^{\text{rec}}(S_f, D_f, u).

Proposition (Deadlock Avoidance). Under the exemption axiom, at least one recovery action remains authorized.

Proof. By the axiom, P_{r+1} \supseteq P_{\min}^{\text{rec}}. By definition, P_{\min}^{\text{rec}} contains \Gamma(a^\star) for every a^\star \in \text{RecPlan}. Hence \Gamma(a^\star) \subseteq P_{r+1}, so a^\star \in \mathcal{A}(P_{r+1}). \square

The control arm of the experiment must also satisfy this axiom. If Z=0 sets P_{r+1} = P^{\text{pre}}_f, then define the control as:

P_{r+1} = P^{\text{pre}}_f \cup P_{\min}^{\text{rec}}

or state that P^{\text{pre}}_f is already recovery-closed.

4.4 Permission-Specific Authority Policy

The authority policy is:

\boxed{
\pi_A: (\hat{\mathbf R}, E^R, C, P^{\text{pre}}) \to P^{\text{post}}
}

For each primitive permission p \in \mathcal{P}_0, grant p if:

\hat r_{\dim(p)} \geq \tau_p(C, E^R),

where \tau_p is a pre-registered, possibly criticality-dependent threshold. This makes authority permission-specific rather than scalar.

A policy \pi_A is recoverability-monotone if:

\hat{\mathbf R}^{(1)} \succeq \hat{\mathbf R}^{(2)} \implies \pi_A(\hat{\mathbf R}^{(1)}, \cdot) \supseteq \pi_A(\hat{\mathbf R}^{(2)}, \cdot),

where \succeq is the componentwise partial order on [0,1]^6. The permission-specific threshold policy is recoverability-monotone. The monotone policy class \Pi_{\text{mono}} is a strict subset of the unconstrained policy class \Pi_{\text{unc}}. Whether \Pi_{\text{mono}} is dominated by \Pi_{\text{unc}} in safety is an open empirical question.

5. Structural Causal Model

5.1 Endogenous and Exogenous Variables

Endogenous variables:

\mathcal{V} = \{F, D, U, S'_r, R^\star, E^R, \hat{\mathbf R}, Z, Z_{\text{rec}}, A_{r+1}, P_{r+1}, X_{r+1}, Y_H\}.

Exogenous variables:

\mathcal{U} = \{U_F, U_D, U_R, U_Z, U_A, U_H\}.

5.2 Structural Equations

F = f_F(U_F),

D = f_D(F, U_D),

(U, S'_r) = f_R(F, D, B_R),

R^\star = f_{R^\star}(F, U, S'_r, B_R, \mathcal{E}),

E^R = f_E(F, U, S'_r),

\hat{\mathbf R} = f_{\hat{\mathbf R}}(S_f, u, S'_r, E^R, U_R),

Z_{\text{rec}} = f_{Z_{\text{rec}}}(\cdot) \quad \text{(randomized)},

Z = f_Z(U_Z) \quad \text{(randomized, exogenous)},

A_{r+1} = \begin{cases} \pi_A(\hat{\mathbf R}, E^R, C, P^{\text{pre}}) & Z = 1 \\ A^{\text{pre}}_f & Z = 0 \end{cases},

P_{r+1} = \psi(A_{r+1}, P^{\text{pre}}),

X_{r+1} = f_X(S_r, P_{r+1}, U_A),

Y_H = f_{Y_H}(X_{r+1}, H).

The verifier equation \hat{\mathbf R} = f_{\hat{\mathbf R}}(S_f, u, S'_r, E^R, U_R) depends only on observables. The latent R^\star appears as a parent of the measurement model only if explicitly stated; it is not an input to \mathcal{V}.

5.3 Causal DAG

The DAG is:

```
   U_F    U_D    U_R                        U_Z    U_A    U_H
    │      │      │                          │      │      │
    ▼      ▼      ▼                          ▼      ▼      ▼
    F ───► D ───► (U,S'_r) ──► R* ──► R̂ ──► A_{r+1} ──► P_{r+1} ──► X_{r+1} ──► Y_H
                    │                  ▲        ▲                                │
                    │                  │        │                                │
                    └──► E^R ──────────┘        Z                                │
                                                │                                ▼
                                                │                                H
                                                │
                                                └── (randomized assignment)
```

The key property is that Z is generated by U_Z and enters A_{r+1} as an exogenous randomizer. It is not downstream of \hat{\mathbf R}. Stratification or blocking by \hat{\mathbf R}, criticality, or pre-failure permission is permitted at the design stage, but assignment remains randomized within blocks.

5.4 Identification Assumptions

· (A1) Consistency: Y_i(Z = z) = Y_i whenever Z_i = z.
· (A2) Positivity: 0 < P(Z = 1 \mid \cdot) < 1.
· (A3) Randomization: Z \perp (F, D, U, S'_r, R^\star, E^R, \hat{\mathbf R}) \mid B_R by design.
· (A4) SUTVA: No interference between trajectories.

Under these assumptions:

\mathbb{E}[Y_H \mid do(Z = z)] = \mathbb{E}[Y_H \mid Z = z].

The primary average treatment effect is:

\boxed{
\text{ATE}_{Y_H} = \mathbb{E}[Y_H \mid do(Z = 1)] - \mathbb{E}[Y_H \mid do(Z = 0)].
}

Under randomization, this is identified by the observed difference in means. The randomized assignment identifies the causal effect of the authority policy itself.

6. Mediation Through Permission State

6.1 Interventional Indirect Effect

The operative mediator is M = P_{r+1}, the permission state. The interventional indirect effect (IIE) is:

\boxed{
\text{IIE} = \mathbb{E}[Y(0, G(1 \mid c))] - \mathbb{E}[Y(0, G(0 \mid c))]
}

where G(a \mid c) is a random draw from the distribution of the mediator M when the exposure is set to a, conditional on covariates C = c. This is identifiable under consistency, positivity, and no unmeasured confounding of the A–M and M–Y relationships conditional on C. The assumption of no exposure-induced confounders of the mediator and outcome, or no mean interaction between exposure-induced confounders and the mediator on the additive scale, is required. Randomization of Z alone does not identify mediation; the M–Y confounding assumptions must be stated explicitly.

6.2 Total Effect Decomposition

The total effect decomposes as:

\text{ATE}_{Y_H} = \text{IDE} + \text{IIE},

where:

\text{IDE} = \mathbb{E}[Y(1, G(1 \mid c))] - \mathbb{E}[Y(0, G(0 \mid c))],

\text{IIE} = \mathbb{E}[Y(0, G(1 \mid c))] - \mathbb{E}[Y(0, G(0 \mid c))].

For a binary mediator, the IIE takes the product form:

\text{IIE} = \int_c \Bigl(\mathbb{E}[Y \mid M = 1, C = c, A = 0] - \mathbb{E}[Y \mid M = 0, C = c, A = 0]\Bigr) \times \Bigl(P(M = 1 \mid C = c, A = 1) - P(M = 1 \mid C = c, A = 0)\Bigr) f_C(c) \, dc.

7. Formal Hypotheses

7.1 Primary Hypothesis

H_1: \text{ATE}_{Y_H} < 0

with continuity constraint:

P_S(Z = 1) \geq P_S(Z = 0) - \delta, \quad \delta = 0.15 \text{ (pre-registered)},

where P_S = \mathbf{1}\{\text{recovery plan } u \text{ completes within budget } B_R \text{ and all invariants are restored}\}.

7.2 Mechanistic Hypothesis

H_2: \text{IIE} \neq 0 \quad \text{and significant},

with mediation path Z \to A_{r+1} \to P_{r+1} \to Y_H. The operative mediator is P_{r+1}.

7.3 Marginal Safety of Additional Authority

Define the pairwise intervention contrast:

\boxed{
\Delta_H(R,C,P_1,P_2) = \mathbb{E}[H \mid do(P = P_2), R, C] - \mathbb{E}[H \mid do(P = P_1), R, C]
}

for nested permission sets P_1 \subsetneq P_2. Three scientific outcomes are permitted: a threshold model where \Delta_H > 0 for some R < \tau(C); a smooth interaction where \Delta_H varies continuously with R and C without a sharp threshold; or no meaningful interaction where \Delta_H is indistinguishable from zero. The threshold model is not forced.

The current design randomizes Z, which changes whether \pi_A is used; it does not directly intervene on P_1 vs P_2. To estimate \Delta_H, a separate randomized permission-set assignment conditional on (\hat{\mathbf R}, C) is required, or a model-based g-formula approach with explicit no-unmeasured-M–Y-confounding assumptions.

7.4 Verified Recovery vs. Retry

H_4: \mathbb{E}[Y_H \mid do(Z_{\text{rec}} = \text{verified})] \neq \mathbb{E}[Y_H \mid do(Z_{\text{rec}} = \text{retry})].

P_S, \mathbb{E}[H], and T_{\text{safe}} are reported separately, not held equal.

7.5 Calibration

H_5: \text{ECE}_d \leq \epsilon_{\text{ECE}} \quad (\epsilon_{\text{ECE}} = 0.05)

for each recoverability dimension d, measured against a ground-truth oracle in the controlled pilot.

7.6 Null Hypothesis

H_0: \text{ATE}_{Y_H} = 0.

8. Experimental Design

8.1 Primary Design: Repeated Micro-Randomized Trial

At every recovery completion r, randomize Z \sim \text{Bernoulli}(1/2):

· Z = 1: P_{r+1} = \pi_A(\hat{\mathbf R}_r, E^R_r, C_{r+1}, P^{\text{pre}}_f)
· Z = 0: P_{r+1} = P^{\text{pre}}_f \cup P_{\min}^{\text{rec}}

Held constant across arms: model, task, failure injection, diagnosis, recovery mechanism, checkpoint, recovery budget, verifier. Only \pi_A varies.

8.2 Secondary Design: Recovery-Method Randomization

For H_4: Z_{\text{rec}} \sim \text{Bernoulli}(1/2), Z_{\text{rec}} \in \{\text{verified}, \text{retry}\}.

8.3 Factorial Design for Bottleneck Identification

Design: \text{Oracle}_D \times \text{Oracle}_R \times \pi_A. Permitted inference rules:

Observation Permitted inference
Oracle advantage over ARCA Estimation (diagnosis and/or \hat{\mathbf R}) may be limiting
No Oracle advantage Estimation may not be limiting under this regime
Adaptive still poor even with Oracle \hat{\mathbf R} The authority policy may be inadequate
Fixed authority outperforms adaptive even with Oracle \hat{\mathbf R} The central R \to A hypothesis is challenged

8.4 Monotonicity Test

Run three policies: \pi_0 = \text{fixed authority}, \pi_1 \in \Pi_{\text{mono}}, \pi_2 \in \Pi_{\text{unc}}. Test whether \pi_2 strictly dominates \pi_1 on (\text{ATE}_{Y_H}, \text{ATE}_{P_S}).

8.5 Independent Variables

Horizon H \in \{1, 5, 10, 25, 50, 100\}; policy \pi \in \{\pi_0, \pi_1, \pi_2\}; criticality C \in \{0.25, 0.5, 0.75\}; recovery budget B_R; randomized assignments Z, Z_{\text{rec}}.

8.6 Dependent Variables

Primary: Y_H = \mathbf{1}\{H > 0\}. Secondary: \mathbb{E}[H], T_{\text{safe}}, T_{\text{recovery}}, P_S, human intervention rate.

8.7 Domains (Pilot)

Controlled recovery tasks (primary; failure injection at known steps); software engineering (Traverse-style benchmark). Computer use and cyber defence are deferred to a subsequent phase.

8.8 Estimator for Repeated Micro-Randomization

Because each trajectory contributes multiple recovery events, the primary estimator is a mixed-effects model:

Y_{ir} = \alpha + \beta Z_{ir} + \gamma^\top X_{ir} + b_i + \epsilon_{ir},

where i indexes trajectory, r indexes recovery event, b_i is a trajectory-level random effect, and \beta is the causal authority effect. Standard errors are trajectory-clustered. Randomization inference is the primary robustness analysis.

However, the simple random-intercept model does not address carryover. Recent advances in micro-randomized trials provide the distal causal excursion effect (DCEE), which quantifies long-term effects of time-varying treatments while marginalizing over most treatment assignments. Doubly robust estimators for causal excursion effects with history adjustment and randomization-probability weighting are now available. If no-carryover is assumed, it must be justified explicitly and tested for sensitivity. The recommended estimator is a doubly robust causal excursion estimator with history adjustment, or a DCEE-based estimator if distal effects are of interest.

8.9 Falsification Criteria

1. \text{ATE}_{Y_H} \geq 0 (H_1 refuted)
2. P_S(Z=1) < P_S(Z=0) - \delta (H_1 refuted by cost)
3. IIE indistinguishable from 0 (H_2 refuted)
4. \text{ECE}_d > \epsilon_{\text{ECE}} (H_5 refuted)
5. \pi_2 strictly dominates \pi_1 on both endpoints (monotonicity imposes cost)

9. Safe Autonomy Envelope

The safe autonomy envelope is:

\mathcal{E}_{\text{safe}} = \{(H, \pi_A, B_R, C, P_0, \hat{\mathbf R}) : P(Y_H = 1) \leq \epsilon\}.

Boundary estimation uses safe Bayesian optimization with Gaussian process confidence bounds. SafeOpt-style algorithms guarantee that the performance of the system never falls below a critical value, defining safety based on the performance function. The structural claim is:

\text{Safety} = f(H, \text{Authority}, \text{Recoverability}, \text{Criticality}, \text{Recovery Budget}),

not merely \text{Safety} = f(\text{model capability}).

10. Power Analysis

Primary endpoint Y_H = \mathbf{1}\{H > 0\} (binary). Based on Rahman et al. (2026) and Barke et al. (2026): p_0 = P(Y_H = 1 \mid Z = 0) \approx 0.25, p_1 = P(Y_H = 1 \mid Z = 1) \approx 0.15. Two-sided test, \alpha = 0.05, power 1 - \beta = 0.80:

n_{\text{per arm}} = \frac{(1.96 + 0.84)^2 \cdot [0.25 \cdot 0.75 + 0.15 \cdot 0.85]}{(0.25 - 0.15)^2} = 247.0.

Total N_{\text{total}} = 494. With m \approx 5 recovery events per trajectory and \rho \approx 0.15:

\text{DE} = 1 + (5 - 1) \cdot 0.15 = 1.6,

N_{\text{cluster-adjusted}} = 494 \times 1.6 = 790.

Number of clusters per arm: c = 790 / (2 \times 5) = 79.

For mediation power, with R^2 \approx 0.10, n \geq 800 events are required unclustered. With DE \approx 1.6, the cluster-adjusted requirement is n_{\text{IIE, cluster-adjusted}} \approx 800 \times 1.6 = 1{,}280 events.

The n \approx 50 pilot is a feasibility study, not a confirmatory efficacy study. Pre-register: \delta = 0.15; \epsilon_{\text{ECE}} = 0.05; \epsilon (harm tolerance); \sigma (severity weights); primary endpoint Y_H; stopping rules; mediation model.

11. Contributions

Theoretical. Three-layer recoverability model: R^\star (latent functional) / \hat{\mathbf R} (estimated vector) / E^R (evidence). Action-permission mapping \Gamma and scoped recovery capability P_{\min}^{\text{rec}}(S, D). Rigorous deadlock avoidance. Permission-specific authority policy \pi_A. Joint intervention for marginal authority harm (H_3). Randomized recovery-method design avoiding post-treatment selection (H_4). Causal estimand \text{ATE}_{Y_H} = \mathbb{E}[Y_H \mid do(Z=1)] - \mathbb{E}[Y_H \mid do(Z=0)]. Open theoretical question: \Pi_{\text{mono}} vs \Pi_{\text{unc}}.

Empirical. First micro-randomized test of R \to A. Interventional mediation estimate through P_{r+1}. Factorial bottleneck identification. Calibration analysis of \hat{\mathbf R}. Safe Autonomy Envelope.

Practical. ARCA reference implementation (minimal pilot scope). Post-failure authority benchmark with controllable failure injection.

12. Control Loop

The control loop is:

\text{Failure} \to \text{Diagnosis} \to \text{Recovery} \to \text{Verification} \to \text{Authority Transition} \to \text{Action} \to \text{Evidence}.

The recovery controller C_R maps (S_f, D_f, E_f, B_R) \to (u, \hat{\mathbf R}_r, E^R_r). The authority controller C_A maps (\hat{\mathbf R}_r, E^R_r, C_{r+1}, P^{\text{pre}}_f) \to P_{r+1}.

13. Status of This Version

This version—Version 5.0—integrates the revised critical advice with recent methodological advances. The central falsifiable claim is:

\boxed{
\text{A post-failure authority policy is causally useful if and only if changing it, while holding recovery fixed, changes safety outcomes.}
}

This is made operational through randomized authority assignment, while explicitly modelling recoverability estimation error and repeated-event dependence. The R \to A mechanism is directly testable through randomized recoverability-signal manipulation (oracle vs. noisy vs. null \hat{\mathbf R}), measured by authority transitions A_{r+1}. The A \to Y link is identified by the primary micro-randomized design. Together, these two designs provide a complete causal test of the recoverability-conditioned authority mechanism.


Appendices

Appendix A: Mathematical Foundations of the Permission Lattice

A.1 Posets, Lattices, and Completeness

Definition A.1 (Partially Ordered Set). A partially ordered set (poset) is a pair (X, \preceq) where X is a set and \preceq is a binary relation on X satisfying:

1. Reflexivity: x \preceq x for all x \in X.
2. Antisymmetry: if x \preceq y and y \preceq x, then x = y.
3. Transitivity: if x \preceq y and y \preceq z, then x \preceq z.

Definition A.2 (Join and Meet). Let (X, \preceq) be a poset and S \subseteq X. An element u \in X is an upper bound of S if s \preceq u for all s \in S. The least upper bound (supremum, join) of S is an upper bound u such that u \preceq v for every upper bound v of S. Dually, l \in X is a lower bound of S if l \preceq s for all s \in S, and the greatest lower bound (infimum, meet) is the largest such lower bound. We write \bigvee S for the join and \bigwedge S for the meet.

Definition A.3 (Lattice). A poset (L, \preceq) is a lattice if every pair x, y \in L has both a join x \vee y and a meet x \wedge y in L. It is a complete lattice if every subset S \subseteq L has both a join \bigvee S and a meet \bigwedge S in L.

Theorem A.1 (The Subset Lattice). Let X be a set and \mathcal{P}(X) its power set. Then (\mathcal{P}(X), \subseteq) is a complete, distributive, and complemented lattice with:

\text{join: } Z \vee Z' = Z \cup Z', \qquad \text{meet: } Z \wedge Z' = Z \cap Z'.

Proof. We verify each lattice axiom.

Partial order. Set inclusion \subseteq is reflexive, antisymmetric, and transitive on \mathcal{P}(X). \checkmark

Join. For Z, Z' \in \mathcal{P}(X), we have Z \subseteq Z \cup Z' and Z' \subseteq Z \cup Z', so Z \cup Z' is an upper bound of \{Z, Z'\}. If Y \in \mathcal{P}(X) satisfies Z \subseteq Y and Z' \subseteq Y, then every element of Z \cup Z' is in Y, so Z \cup Z' \subseteq Y. Thus Z \cup Z' is the least upper bound. \checkmark

Meet. Dually, Z \cap Z' \subseteq Z and Z \cap Z' \subseteq Z', so Z \cap Z' is a lower bound. If Y \subseteq Z and Y \subseteq Z', then every element of Y is in both Z and Z', so Y \subseteq Z \cap Z'. Thus Z \cap Z' is the greatest lower bound. \checkmark

Completeness. For an arbitrary family \{Z_i\}_{i \in I} \subseteq \mathcal{P}(X), the union \bigcup_{i \in I} Z_i satisfies the supremum conditions and the intersection \bigcap_{i \in I} Z_i satisfies the infimum conditions. Hence (\mathcal{P}(X), \subseteq) is a complete lattice. \checkmark

Distributivity. Elementary set theory gives Z \cup (Z' \cap Z'') = (Z \cup Z') \cap (Z \cup Z'') and Z \cap (Z' \cup Z'') = (Z \cap Z') \cup (Z \cap Z''). \checkmark

Complemented. For each Z \in \mathcal{P}(X), let \overline{Z} = X \setminus Z. Then Z \cup \overline{Z} = X = \top and Z \cap \overline{Z} = \varnothing = \bot. \square

Corollary A.1. The permission lattice (\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{P}_0), \subseteq) is a complete, distributive, complemented lattice with \bot = \varnothing, \top = \mathcal{P}_0, meet = \cap, join = \cup.

A.2 Scoped Recovery Capability as a Join

Definition A.4 (Recovery Plan Action Set). Let \text{RecPlan}(S_f, D_f, u) be the finite set of actions in the verified recovery plan u, with cardinality |\text{RecPlan}| = n < \infty.

Proposition A.1. The scoped recovery capability

P_{\min}^{\text{rec}}(S_f, D_f, u) = \bigcup_{a \in \text{RecPlan}(S_f, D_f, u)} \Gamma(a)

is the join of the permission sets required by the recovery plan actions:

P_{\min}^{\text{rec}} = \bigvee_{a \in \text{RecPlan}} \Gamma(a).

Proof. By Definition A.2, the join of the finite family \{\Gamma(a)\}_{a \in \text{RecPlan}} is its least upper bound. In the subset lattice, the least upper bound is the union. Hence \bigvee_{a \in \text{RecPlan}} \Gamma(a) = \bigcup_{a \in \text{RecPlan}} \Gamma(a) = P_{\min}^{\text{rec}}. \square

A.3 Deadlock Avoidance: Extended Proof

Theorem A.2 (Deadlock Avoidance). Under the recovery-critical exemption axiom P_{r+1} \supseteq P_{\min}^{\text{rec}}(S_f, D_f, u), at least one recovery action remains authorized.

Proof. By the axiom, P_{r+1} \supseteq P_{\min}^{\text{rec}}(S_f, D_f, u). By Definition A.4 and Proposition A.1, P_{\min}^{\text{rec}} = \bigcup_{a \in \text{RecPlan}} \Gamma(a). Since \text{RecPlan} is non-empty, choose any a^\star \in \text{RecPlan}. Then

\Gamma(a^\star) \subseteq \bigcup_{a \in \text{RecPlan}} \Gamma(a) = P_{\min}^{\text{rec}} \subseteq P_{r+1}.

By Definition 4.3, a^\star \in \mathcal{A}(P_{r+1}). Hence the authorized action set is non-empty. \square

Corollary A.2. Deadlock avoidance does not require global write authority. The exemption is scoped to the recovery plan's action requirements.

A.4 Monotonicity of the Authority Policy

Definition A.5 (Componentwise Partial Order). For \hat{\mathbf{R}}^{(1)}, \hat{\mathbf{R}}^{(2)} \in [0,1]^6:

\hat{\mathbf{R}}^{(1)} \succeq \hat{\mathbf{R}}^{(2)} \iff \hat{r}^{(1)}_d \geq \hat{r}^{(2)}_d \quad \forall d \in \{\text{state}, \ldots, \text{reversibility}\}.

Definition A.6 (Recoverability-Monotone Policy). A policy \pi_A is recoverability-monotone if:

\hat{\mathbf{R}}^{(1)} \succeq \hat{\mathbf{R}}^{(2)} \implies \pi_A(\hat{\mathbf{R}}^{(1)}, \cdot) \supseteq \pi_A(\hat{\mathbf{R}}^{(2)}, \cdot).

Proposition A.2. The permission-specific threshold policy

\pi_A(\hat{\mathbf{R}}, E^R, C, P^{\text{pre}}) = \{p \in \mathcal{P}_0 : \hat{r}_{\dim(p)} \geq \tau_p(C, E^R)\}

is recoverability-monotone.

Proof. Let \hat{\mathbf{R}}^{(1)} \succeq \hat{\mathbf{R}}^{(2)}. For any p \in \pi_A(\hat{\mathbf{R}}^{(2)}, \cdot), we have \hat{r}^{(2)}_{\dim(p)} \geq \tau_p(C, E^R). By componentwise order, \hat{r}^{(1)}_{\dim(p)} \geq \hat{r}^{(2)}_{\dim(p)} \geq \tau_p(C, E^R). Hence p \in \pi_A(\hat{\mathbf{R}}^{(1)}, \cdot). Therefore \pi_A(\hat{\mathbf{R}}^{(2)}, \cdot) \subseteq \pi_A(\hat{\mathbf{R}}^{(1)}, \cdot). \square

A.5 Non-Amplifying Authorization on a Product Lattice

Definition A.7 (Authority Lattice). Let \mathrm{Auth} be a partially ordered set representing authorization levels from \bot to \top.

Definition A.8 (Delegation Relation). The delegation relation is a function:

\delta: \mathcal{H} \cup \mathbf{A} \times \mathbf{A} \times \mathcal{T}_{\text{task}} \times \mathcal{X} \to \mathrm{Auth} \cup \{\bot\},

where \delta(p, \mathcal{A}, \tau, x) = \alpha means principal p delegates authority level \alpha to agent \mathcal{A} for task type \tau in context x.

Definition A.9 (Policy Cap). For each agent \mathcal{A}, task type \tau, and execution context x, the organizational policy defines a policy cap \Gamma(\mathcal{A}, \tau, x) \in \mathrm{Auth}.

Definition A.10 (Effective Authority). The effective authority is:

\mathrm{Auth}(\mathcal{A}, \tau, x) = \Sigma(\mathcal{A}, \tau, x) \wedge \Gamma(\mathcal{A}, \tau, x),

where \Sigma is the source authority.

Theorem A.3 (Non-Amplifying Authorization). Under Definition A.10:

\mathrm{Auth}(\mathcal{A}, \tau, x) \preceq \Gamma(\mathcal{A}, \tau, x).

Proof. By definition of the meet in a lattice, a \wedge b \preceq b for all a, b. Setting a = \Sigma(\mathcal{A}, \tau, x) and b = \Gamma(\mathcal{A}, \tau, x):

\mathrm{Auth}(\mathcal{A}, \tau, x) = \Sigma(\mathcal{A}, \tau, x) \wedge \Gamma(\mathcal{A}, \tau, x) \preceq \Gamma(\mathcal{A}, \tau, x).

\square

Definition A.11 (Faceted Authority Product Lattice). Let the authority space be a product lattice

\mathcal{L} = \prod_{i=1}^{7} L_i,

where L_1 = scope, L_2 = spend, L_3 = depth, L_4 = time, L_5 = reputation, L_6 = values, L_7 = reversibility, each equipped with a partial order \preceq_i. The product order is

\mathbf{a} \preceq \mathbf{b} \iff a_i \preceq_i b_i \quad \forall i \in \{1, \ldots, 7\}.

Theorem A.4 (Attenuation-Only Delegation). Any delegation function d: \mathcal{L} \to \mathcal{L} that is monotone and satisfies d(\mathbf{a}) \preceq \mathbf{a} for all \mathbf{a} \in \mathcal{L} cannot amplify authority in any facet.

Proof. Let \mathbf{a} \in \mathcal{L}. By hypothesis, d(\mathbf{a}) \preceq \mathbf{a}. Componentwise, d(\mathbf{a})_i \preceq_i a_i for all i. Hence no facet is amplified. If d is monotone, then \mathbf{a} \preceq \mathbf{b} \implies d(\mathbf{a}) \preceq d(\mathbf{b}), preserving the lattice structure. \square

Appendix B: Causal Identification

B.1 Identification of the Primary ATE

Theorem B.1 (Identification under Randomization). Suppose:

1. Consistency: Y_i(Z = z) = Y_i whenever Z_i = z.
2. Positivity: 0 < P(Z = 1 \mid \cdot) < 1.
3. Randomization: Z \perp (F, D, U, S'_r, R^\star, E^R, \hat{\mathbf{R}}) \mid B_R by design.
4. SUTVA: No interference between trajectories.

Then:

\mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(Z = z)] = \mathbb{E}[Y \mid Z = z].

Proof. By the law of total expectation and consistency:

\mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(Z = z)] = \mathbb{E}[Y(z)].

We must show \mathbb{E}[Y(z)] = \mathbb{E}[Y \mid Z = z]. We have:

\mathbb{E}[Y \mid Z = z] = \mathbb{E}[Y(z) \mid Z = z] \quad \text{(consistency)}.

By randomization, Z \perp Y(z), so:

\mathbb{E}[Y(z) \mid Z = z] = \mathbb{E}[Y(z)].

Therefore \mathbb{E}[Y \mid Z = z] = \mathbb{E}[Y(z)] = \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(Z = z)]. \square

Corollary B.1. The ATE \text{ATE}_Y = \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(Z = 1)] - \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(Z = 0)] is identified by the observed difference in means:

\text{ATE}_Y = \mathbb{E}[Y \mid Z = 1] - \mathbb{E}[Y \mid Z = 0].

B.2 Stratified Randomization

Definition B.1 (Stratified Randomization). Partition trajectories into strata B \in \mathcal{B} based on pre-failure covariates (\hat{\mathbf{R}}^{\text{pre}}, C^{\text{pre}}, P^{\text{pre}}). Within each stratum B, independently randomize Z \sim \text{Bernoulli}(1/2).

Theorem B.2. Under stratified randomization:

\mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(Z = z)] = \sum_{B \in \mathcal{B}} \mathbb{E}[Y \mid Z = z, B] \cdot P(B).

Proof. By the law of total expectation:

\mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(Z = z)] = \sum_{B \in \mathcal{B}} \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(Z = z), B] \cdot P(B \mid do(Z = z)).

Since randomization is within strata and Z is randomized, P(B \mid do(Z = z)) = P(B). Within each stratum, Theorem B.1 applies:

\mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(Z = z), B] = \mathbb{E}[Y \mid Z = z, B].

Therefore the result follows. \square

B.3 Finite-Sample Unbiasedness

Proposition B.1. The difference-in-means estimator

\hat{\text{ATE}}_Y = \frac{1}{n_1} \sum_{i: Z_i = 1} Y_i - \frac{1}{n_0} \sum_{i: Z_i = 0} Y_i

is unbiased for \text{ATE}_Y.

Proof. Conditional on \{Z_i\}:

\mathbb{E}[\hat{\text{ATE}}_Y \mid \{Z_i\}] = \frac{1}{n_1} \sum_{i: Z_i = 1} \mathbb{E}[Y_i \mid Z_i = 1] - \frac{1}{n_0} \sum_{i: Z_i = 0} \mathbb{E}[Y_i \mid Z_i = 0].

By Theorem B.1, \mathbb{E}[Y_i \mid Z_i = z] = \mathbb{E}[Y_i(z)]. Therefore:

\mathbb{E}[\hat{\text{ATE}}_Y \mid \{Z_i\}] = \frac{1}{n_1} \sum_{i: Z_i = 1} \mathbb{E}[Y_i(1)] - \frac{1}{n_0} \sum_{i: Z_i = 0} \mathbb{E}[Y_i(0)].

Taking expectations over \{Z_i\} and noting \mathbb{E}[\mathbf{1}\{Z_i = 1\}] = 1/2:

\mathbb{E}[\hat{\text{ATE}}_Y] = \mathbb{E}[Y_i(1)] - \mathbb{E}[Y_i(0)] = \text{ATE}_Y.

\square

B.4 Positivity and Overlap Diagnostics

Definition B.2 (Propensity Score). The propensity score is e(x) = P(Z = 1 \mid X = x).

Proposition B.2 (Overlap Condition). Identification of the ATE requires 0 < e(x) < 1 for all x in the support of X. Under Bernoulli(1/2) randomization, e(x) = 1/2 for all x, so overlap is guaranteed by design.

Proof. Under Z \sim \text{Bernoulli}(1/2), e(x) = 1/2 \in (0, 1) for all x. \square

Appendix C: Interventional Mediation Analysis

C.1 Interventional Effects

Definition C.1 (Interventional Effects). Let G(a \mid c) be a random draw from the distribution of the mediator M when the exposure is set to a, conditional on covariates C = c. The interventional direct and indirect effects are:

\text{IDE}(a, a^*) = \mathbb{E}[Y(a, G(a^* \mid c))] - \mathbb{E}[Y(a^*, G(a^* \mid c))],

\text{IIE}(a, a^*) = \mathbb{E}[Y(a, G(a \mid c))] - \mathbb{E}[Y(a, G(a^* \mid c))],

\text{ITE}(a, a^*) = \text{IDE}(a, a^*) + \text{IIE}(a, a^*).

Theorem C.1 (Identification of the Interventional Indirect Effect). Under consistency, positivity, and no unmeasured confounding of the A–M and M–Y relationships conditional on C:

\mathbb{E}[Y(a, G(a^* \mid c))] = \int_c \int_m \int_l \mathbb{E}[Y \mid A = a, M = m, L = l, C = c] \, f_{L \mid A, C}(l \mid a, c) \, f_{M \mid A, C}(m \mid a^*, c) \, f_C(c) \, d\mu_L(l) \, d\mu_M(m) \, d\mu_C(c).

Proof. By consistency and the absence of unmeasured confounding:

\mathbb{E}[Y(a, G(a^* \mid c))] = \int \mathbb{E}[Y \mid A = a, M = m, L = l, C = c] \, dP(m, l \mid a, a^*, c).

Under the assumption that M(a^*) is independent of L(a) given A = a, C = c, the joint distribution factorizes:

dP(m, l \mid a, a^*, c) = f_{M \mid A, C}(m \mid a^*, c) \, f_{L \mid A, C}(l \mid a, c) \, d\mu_M(m) \, d\mu_L(l).

Substituting and integrating over C yields the result. \square

C.2 Application to the Authority Mechanism

In the present framework:

· A = Z (randomized authority assignment),
· M = P_{r+1} (permission state),
· Y = Y_H (harm indicator).

The interventional indirect effect through permission state is:

\text{IIE} = \mathbb{E}\bigl[Y(1, P_{r+1}(1)) - Y(1, P_{r+1}(0))\bigr].

Theorem C.2 (Total Effect Decomposition). The total effect decomposes as:

\text{ATE}_Y = \text{IDE} + \text{IIE},

where

\text{IDE} = \mathbb{E}[Y(1, G(1 \mid c))] - \mathbb{E}[Y(0, G(0 \mid c))],

\text{IIE} = \mathbb{E}[Y(0, G(1 \mid c))] - \mathbb{E}[Y(0, G(0 \mid c))].

Proof. By the law of total expectation:

\text{ATE}_Y = \mathbb{E}[Y(1, G(1 \mid c))] - \mathbb{E}[Y(0, G(0 \mid c))].

Add and subtract \mathbb{E}[Y(0, G(1 \mid c))]:

\text{ATE}_Y = \underbrace{\mathbb{E}[Y(1, G(1 \mid c))] - \mathbb{E}[Y(0, G(1 \mid c))]}_{\text{IDE}} + \underbrace{\mathbb{E}[Y(0, G(1 \mid c))] - \mathbb{E}[Y(0, G(0 \mid c))]}_{\text{IIE}}.

\square

C.3 Binary Mediator Product Method

When M = P_{r+1} is binary, the IIE takes the product form:

\text{IIE} = \int_c \Bigl(\mathbb{E}[Y \mid M = 1, C = c, A = 0] - \mathbb{E}[Y \mid M = 0, C = c, A = 0]\Bigr) \times \Bigl(P(M = 1 \mid C = c, A = 1) - P(M = 1 \mid C = c, A = 0)\Bigr) f_C(c) \, dc.

Proposition C.1. If the outcome model is a generalized linear model

\mathbb{E}[Y \mid M = m, C = c, A = 0] = g^{-1}(\beta_1 c + \beta_2 m + \beta_3 c m),

then the IIE is estimated by:

\hat{\text{IIE}} = \frac{1}{n_1} \sum_{i: Z_i = 1} g^{-1}(\hat{\beta}_1 c_i + \hat{\beta}_2 m_i + \hat{\beta}_3 c_i m_i) - \frac{1}{n_0} \sum_{i: Z_i = 0} g^{-1}(\hat{\beta}_1 c_i + \hat{\beta}_2 m_i + \hat{\beta}_3 c_i m_i).

Proof. This follows from the product method formula and consistency of the GLM parameter estimates. The first term estimates \mathbb{E}[Y(0, M(1))] and the second estimates \mathbb{E}[Y(0, M(0))]. \square

C.4 Sensitivity to Unmeasured Mediator–Outcome Confounding

Definition C.2 (Sensitivity Parameter). Let \rho denote the correlation between the unmeasured mediator–outcome confounder U and the outcome Y conditional on (A, M, C), and let \eta denote the correlation between U and the mediator M conditional on (A, C). The bias in the IIE estimate is bounded by:

|\text{Bias}(\hat{\text{IIE}})| \leq |\rho \cdot \eta| \cdot \sigma_Y \cdot \sigma_M.

Proposition C.2. If either \rho = 0 or \eta = 0, the IIE is identified without further assumptions.

Proof. If \rho = 0, then U does not affect Y conditional on (A, M, C), so the M–Y relationship is unconfounded. If \eta = 0, then U does not affect M conditional on (A, C), so the mediator is exogenous. In either case, the standard identification assumptions hold. \square

Appendix D: Repeated Micro-Randomization and Clustering

D.1 Clustered Data Structure

Definition D.1 (Trajectory-Recovery Structure). Let i = 1, \ldots, N index trajectories. Let r = 1, \ldots, m_i index recovery events within trajectory i. The total number of recovery events is M = \sum_{i=1}^N m_i.

Assumption D.1 (Exchangeability within Trajectories). Recovery events within the same trajectory share trajectory-level random effects b_i:

Y_{ir} = \alpha + \beta Z_{ir} + \gamma^\top X_{ir} + b_i + \epsilon_{ir},

where b_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_b^2) and \epsilon_{ir} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_\epsilon^2) are independent.

D.2 Design Effect Derivation

Theorem D.1 (Design Effect for Clustered Outcomes). Let \rho be the intracluster correlation coefficient:

\rho = \frac{\sigma_b^2}{\sigma_b^2 + \sigma_\epsilon^2}.

For a cluster of average size m, the design effect is:

\text{DE} = 1 + (m - 1)\rho.

Proof. Consider the variance of the cluster mean \bar{Y}_i = \frac{1}{m} \sum_{r=1}^m Y_{ir}. Under the random effects model:

\text{Var}(\bar{Y}_i) = \text{Var}\left(\frac{1}{m} \sum_{r=1}^m (\alpha + \beta Z_{ir} + \gamma^\top X_{ir} + b_i + \epsilon_{ir})\right).

Since b_i is common to all observations in cluster i:

\text{Var}(\bar{Y}_i) = \sigma_b^2 + \frac{\sigma_\epsilon^2}{m}.

The variance under independent sampling would be:

\text{Var}_{\text{ind}}(\bar{Y}_i) = \frac{\sigma_b^2 + \sigma_\epsilon^2}{m}.

The design effect is the ratio:

\text{DE} = \frac{\text{Var}(\bar{Y}_i)}{\text{Var}_{\text{ind}}(\bar{Y}_i)} = \frac{\sigma_b^2 + \sigma_\epsilon^2/m}{(\sigma_b^2 + \sigma_\epsilon^2)/m} = \frac{m\sigma_b^2 + \sigma_\epsilon^2}{\sigma_b^2 + \sigma_\epsilon^2}.

Substituting \rho = \sigma_b^2/(\sigma_b^2 + \sigma_\epsilon^2):

\text{DE} = m\rho + (1 - \rho) = 1 + (m - 1)\rho.

\square

D.3 Sample Size Calculation

Theorem D.2 (Sample Size for Clustered Binary Outcomes). For a two-sided test with significance level \alpha and power 1 - \beta, the required number of clusters per arm is:

c = \frac{(z_{\alpha/2} + z_\beta)^2 \cdot [\pi_0(1 - \pi_0) + \pi_1(1 - \pi_1)]}{m(\pi_0 - \pi_1)^2} \cdot \text{DE},

where \pi_0 and \pi_1 are the event probabilities in the control and treatment arms.

Proof. The standard sample size formula for binary outcomes under individual randomization is:

n_{\text{ind}} = \frac{(z_{\alpha/2} + z_\beta)^2 \cdot [\pi_0(1 - \pi_0) + \pi_1(1 - \pi_1)]}{(\pi_0 - \pi_1)^2}.

Under cluster randomization with cluster size m, the effective sample size is n_{\text{eff}} = n_{\text{ind}} / \text{DE}. The number of clusters required per arm is c = n_{\text{eff}} / m. \square

D.4 Numerical Application

Using p_0 = 0.25, p_1 = 0.15, \alpha = 0.05, power = 0.80, m = 5, \rho = 0.15:

n_{\text{per arm}} = \frac{(1.96 + 0.84)^2 \cdot [0.25 \cdot 0.75 + 0.15 \cdot 0.85]}{(0.25 - 0.15)^2} = \frac{7.84 \cdot 0.315}{0.01} = 247.0.

Total N_{\text{total}} = 494. Design effect:

\text{DE} = 1 + (5 - 1) \cdot 0.15 = 1.6.

Cluster-adjusted:

N_{\text{cluster-adjusted}} = 494 \times 1.6 = 790.4 \approx 790.

Number of clusters per arm:

c = \frac{790.4}{2 \times 5} = 79.04 \approx 80.

For mediation power, with R^2 \approx 0.10, n \geq 800 events unclustered. With DE = 1.6:

n_{\text{IIE, cluster-adjusted}} \approx 800 \times 1.6 = 1{,}280 \text{ events}.

D.5 Randomization Inference

Definition D.2 (Randomization Distribution). Under the sharp null H_0^{\text{sharp}}: Y_{ir}(1) = Y_{ir}(0) for all i, r, the randomization distribution of any test statistic T(\mathbf{Z}, \mathbf{Y}) is obtained by permuting the treatment assignments \mathbf{Z} according to the randomization scheme.

Proposition D.1 (Validity of Randomization Inference). Under the sharp null and the randomization scheme, the p-value

p = P_{\mathbf{Z} \sim \pi}\bigl(T(\mathbf{Z}, \mathbf{Y}) \geq T(\mathbf{Z}^{\text{obs}}, \mathbf{Y})\bigr)

is exact in finite samples.

Proof. Under the sharp null, the potential outcomes are fixed and the only source of randomness is the treatment assignment \mathbf{Z}, which follows the known randomization distribution \pi. The test statistic T(\mathbf{Z}, \mathbf{Y}) is a function of the observed outcomes and the assignment. Since the assignment is randomized, the probability integral transform gives an exact p-value. \square

D.6 Mixed-Effects Estimator

Definition D.3 (Mixed-Effects Model). The primary estimator is:

Y_{ir} = \alpha + \beta Z_{ir} + \gamma^\top X_{ir} + b_i + \epsilon_{ir},

with b_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_b^2), \epsilon_{ir} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_\epsilon^2).

Proposition D.2 (Consistency of the Mixed-Effects Estimator). Under Assumption D.1 and the identification assumptions of Theorem B.1, the GLS estimator \hat{\beta} is consistent for the causal authority effect.

Proof. The mixed-effects model is a correctly specified model for the conditional mean \mathbb{E}[Y_{ir} \mid Z_{ir}, X_{ir}, b_i]. Under randomization, Z_{ir} is independent of b_i and \epsilon_{ir}. The GLS estimator is consistent for the fixed effect \beta under standard regularity conditions for mixed models. \square

D.7 Causal Excursion Effects for Micro-Randomized Trials

Definition D.4 (Causal Excursion Effect). Let H_{ir} denote the history up to recovery event r in trajectory i, and let A_{ir} denote the treatment indicator. The causal excursion effect is:

\beta_{ir} = \log \frac{\mathbb{E}[Y_{ir}(A_{ir} = 1) \mid H_{ir}, A_{ir} = 1]}{\mathbb{E}[Y_{ir}(A_{ir} = 0) \mid H_{ir}, A_{ir} = 1]},

where the conditioning on A_{ir} = 1 ensures positivity.

Definition D.5 (Distal Causal Excursion Effect). The distal causal excursion effect for outcome Y_{i, r+k} at lag k is:

\beta_{ir}^{(k)} = \log \frac{\mathbb{E}[Y_{i, r+k}(A_{ir} = 1) \mid H_{ir}, A_{ir} = 1]}{\mathbb{E}[Y_{i, r+k}(A_{ir} = 0) \mid H_{ir}, A_{ir} = 1]}.

Proposition D.3 (Doubly Robust Estimator). Under the randomization assumption P(A_{ir} = 1 \mid H_{ir}) = p_{ir}, the doubly robust estimator

\hat{\beta} = \frac{\sum_{i, r} W_{ir} (A_{ir} - p_{ir}) (Y_{ir} - \hat{m}(H_{ir}))}{\sum_{i, r} W_{ir} p_{ir} (1 - p_{ir})}

is consistent for \beta if either the outcome model \hat{m}(H_{ir}) or the treatment probability model p_{ir} is correctly specified, where W_{ir} are weights.

Proof. The estimator combines the outcome regression and the inverse probability weighting. By the standard theory of doubly robust estimation, consistency holds if either nuisance model is correct. \square

Appendix E: Monotonicity and Constrained MDPs

E.1 The Constrained MDP Framework

Definition E.1 (Constrained MDP). A Constrained Markov Decision Process is a tuple (\mathcal{S}, \mathcal{A}, P, r, c, \gamma, d), where \mathcal{S} is the state space, \mathcal{A} the action space, P: \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A} \to \Delta(\mathcal{S}) the transition kernel, r: \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A} \to \mathbb{R} the reward, c: \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A} \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} the cost, \gamma \in [0, 1) the discount factor, and d \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} the cost threshold. The goal is to find \pi: \mathcal{S} \to \Delta(\mathcal{A}) maximizing:

J(\pi) = \mathbb{E}_\pi\left[\sum_{t=0}^\infty \gamma^t r(S_t, A_t)\right]

subject to:

C(\pi) = \mathbb{E}_\pi\left[\sum_{t=0}^\infty \gamma^t c(S_t, A_t)\right] \leq d.

E.2 Monotonicity of the Safe Policy Set

Definition E.2 (Safe Policy Set). For a cost threshold d:

\Pi_{\text{safe}}(d) = \{\pi : C(\pi) \leq d\}.

Proposition E.1 (Monotonicity of the Safe Policy Set). If d_1 \leq d_2, then \Pi_{\text{safe}}(d_1) \subseteq \Pi_{\text{safe}}(d_2).

Proof. Let \pi \in \Pi_{\text{safe}}(d_1). Then C(\pi) \leq d_1 \leq d_2, so \pi \in \Pi_{\text{safe}}(d_2). \square

E.3 Nesting of Policy Classes

Definition E.3 (Monotone Policy Class). The recoverability-monotone policy class is:

\Pi_{\text{mono}} = \{\pi_A : \hat{\mathbf{R}}^{(1)} \succeq \hat{\mathbf{R}}^{(2)} \implies \pi_A(\hat{\mathbf{R}}^{(1)}, \cdot) \supseteq \pi_A(\hat{\mathbf{R}}^{(2)}, \cdot)\}.

Definition E.4 (Unconstrained Policy Class). The unconstrained policy class \Pi_{\text{unc}} contains all measurable \pi_A satisfying the recovery-critical exemption.

Theorem E.1 (Nesting of Policy Classes). \Pi_{\text{mono}} \subseteq \Pi_{\text{unc}}.

Proof. Every monotone policy satisfies the recovery-critical exemption by definition of \Pi_{\text{unc}}. The inclusion is strict because there exist policies satisfying the exemption that are not monotone. \square

Open Question E.1. Is \Pi_{\text{mono}} dominated by \Pi_{\text{unc}} in safety, or does monotonicity impose a cost? This is tested empirically through \pi_1 \in \Pi_{\text{mono}} and \pi_2 \in \Pi_{\text{unc}}.

E.4 Safety Guarantee via Markov's Inequality

Theorem E.2 (Safety Guarantee). Let \pi be a policy satisfying C(\pi) \leq d. Then:

P(\text{safety violation}) \leq \frac{C(\pi)}{d_{\max}} \leq \frac{d}{d_{\max}},

where d_{\max} is the maximum possible cost.

Proof. By Markov's inequality, for any non-negative random variable X with \mathbb{E}[X] \leq d:

P(X \geq d_{\max}) \leq \frac{\mathbb{E}[X]}{d_{\max}} \leq \frac{d}{d_{\max}}.

Setting X = C(\pi) gives the result. \square

E.5 Lagrangian Approach

Definition E.5 (Lagrangian). For multiplier \lambda \geq 0, the Lagrangian is:

\mathcal{L}(\pi, \lambda) = J(\pi) - \lambda (C(\pi) - d).

Proposition E.2 (Lagrangian Saddle Point). Under convexity of J and C in \pi, there exists \lambda^* \geq 0 such that:

\min_{\pi} \max_{\lambda \geq 0} \mathcal{L}(\pi, \lambda) = \max_{\lambda \geq 0} \min_{\pi} \mathcal{L}(\pi, \lambda).

Proof. This is the standard saddle-point condition for convex optimization with convex constraints. \square

Appendix F: Safe Autonomy Envelope and Bayesian Optimization

F.1 The Safe Set

Definition F.1 (Safe Autonomy Envelope). The safe autonomy envelope is:

\mathcal{E}_{\text{safe}} = \{(H, \pi_A, B_R, C, P_0, \hat{\mathbf{R}}) : P(Y_H = 1) \leq \epsilon\}.

Definition F.2 (Largest Reachable Safe Set). The largest reachable safe set from a seed point \mathbf{x}_0 is:

S_\epsilon(\mathbf{x}_0) = \{\mathbf{x} : \exists \text{ a safe path from } \mathbf{x}_0 \text{ to } \mathbf{x}\},

where safety is defined by high-probability confidence bounds on the constraint function.

F.2 Gaussian Process Confidence Bounds

Definition F.3 (Gaussian Process Model). Let f \sim \mathcal{GP}(\mu, k). The posterior given \mathcal{D}_n = \{(\mathbf{x}_i, y_i)\}_{i=1}^n is f \mid \mathcal{D}_n \sim \mathcal{GP}(\mu_n, k_n), where:

\mu_n(\mathbf{x}) = \mu(\mathbf{x}) + \mathbf{k}_n(\mathbf{x})^\top (\mathbf{K}_n + \sigma^2 I)^{-1} (\mathbf{y}_n - \mu(\mathbf{x}_n)),

k_n(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{x}') = k(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{x}') - \mathbf{k}_n(\mathbf{x})^\top (\mathbf{K}_n + \sigma^2 I)^{-1} \mathbf{k}_n(\mathbf{x}').

Definition F.4 (Confidence Bounds). With confidence parameter \beta_n:

\text{UCB}_n(\mathbf{x}) = \mu_n(\mathbf{x}) + \beta_n^{1/2} \sigma_n(\mathbf{x}),

\text{LCB}_n(\mathbf{x}) = \mu_n(\mathbf{x}) - \beta_n^{1/2} \sigma_n(\mathbf{x}).

Theorem F.1 (Safety Guarantee). If the true constraint function f satisfies f(\mathbf{x}) \geq h for all \mathbf{x} in the true safe set, and the confidence bounds hold with probability at least 1 - \delta, then:

S_n = \{\mathbf{x} : \text{LCB}_n(\mathbf{x}) \geq h\}

is a subset of the true safe set with probability at least 1 - \delta.

Proof. If \text{LCB}_n(\mathbf{x}) \geq h and the confidence bound holds, then f(\mathbf{x}) \geq \text{LCB}_n(\mathbf{x}) \geq h. Therefore \mathbf{x} is in the true safe set. \square

F.3 Safe Bayesian Optimization

Algorithm F.1 (SafeOpt-style Optimization).

```
Input: Initial safe set S_0, GP prior, safety threshold h
For t = 1, 2, ..., T:
    1. Fit GP to observations D_{t-1}
    2. Compute confidence bounds UCB_t, LCB_t
    3. Update safe set: S_t = {x : LCB_t(x) >= h}
    4. Compute potential expanders: E_t = {x in S_t : UCB_t(x) >= h}
    5. Select x_t = argmax_{x in E_t} (UCB_t(x) - max_{x' in S_t} LCB_t(x'))
    6. Evaluate f(x_t) with noise
    7. Update D_t = D_{t-1} ∪ {(x_t, y_t)}
```

Proposition F.1. The boundary of the safe autonomy envelope can be estimated by Bayesian optimization of g(H, \pi_A, B_R, C, P_0, \hat{\mathbf{R}}) = P(Y_H = 1) - \epsilon.

Proof. The safe set is \{\mathbf{x} : g(\mathbf{x}) \leq 0\}. The boundary is \{\mathbf{x} : g(\mathbf{x}) = 0\}. Safe Bayesian optimization locates points where g crosses the threshold \epsilon, with confidence bounds ensuring evaluation points remain in the safe set with high probability. \square

Appendix G: Calibration Theory

G.1 Definitions

Definition G.1 (Binary Calibration). Let R^{\star,\text{bin}}_{r,d} = \mathbf{1}\{R^\star_{r,d} \geq \theta_d\} for a pre-registered threshold \theta_d. The verifier \hat{r}_{r,d} is calibrated if:

P(R^{\star,\text{bin}}_{r,d} = 1 \mid \hat{r}_{r,d} = q) = q \quad \forall q \in [0, 1].

Definition G.2 (Expected Calibration Error). For a partition of [0, 1] into M bins B_1, \ldots, B_M:

\text{ECE}_d = \sum_{m=1}^M \frac{|B_{m,d}|}{n} \left| \text{acc}(B_{m,d}) - \text{conf}(B_{m,d}) \right|,

where \text{acc}(B_{m,d}) = \frac{1}{|B_{m,d}|} \sum_{i \in B_{m,d}} R^{\star,\text{bin}}_{i,d} and \text{conf}(B_{m,d}) = \frac{1}{|B_{m,d}|} \sum_{i \in B_{m,d}} \hat{r}_{i,d}.

Definition G.3 (Continuous Multidimensional ECE). The continuous multidimensional ECE is:

M(f) = \sum_{y \in \mathcal{Y}} p(y) \mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x} \sim p(\mathbf{x})} \left[ \| \mathbb{E}[\mathbf{y} \mid \mathbf{x}]_y - f_y(\mathbf{x}) \| \right].

G.2 Local Calibration

Definition G.4 (Local Calibration). A model f is locally calibrated on D if for all \mathbf{x}_i \in D:

\| \hat{\mathbf{p}}_i - \hat{\theta}(\mathbf{y}_i \mid \mathbf{x}_i) \|_1 \leq \epsilon,

where \hat{\mathbf{p}}_i = f(\mathbf{x}_i) and \hat{\theta}(\mathbf{y}_i \mid \mathbf{x}_i) is a kernel estimate of the conditional distribution.

Theorem G.1 (Continuous MECE under Local Calibration). If f satisfies local calibration, then under standard regularity conditions there exists k \in (0, 1] such that:

M(f) \leq \epsilon \cdot k.

Proof. Rewrite M(f) by marginalizing over input and output:

M(f) = \int_{\mathcal{X}} \left[ \sum_{y \in \mathcal{Y}} p(y) \| \mathbb{E}[\mathbf{y} \mid \mathbf{x}]_y - f_y(\mathbf{x}) \| \right] p(\mathbf{x}) d\mathbf{x}.

By the triangle inequality:

\| \mathbb{E}[\mathbf{y} \mid \mathbf{x}]_y - f_y(\mathbf{x}) \| \leq \| \hat{\theta}(\mathbf{y} \mid \mathbf{x}) - f(\mathbf{x}) \|_1 + \| \mathbb{E}[\mathbf{y} \mid \mathbf{x}] - \hat{\theta}(\mathbf{y} \mid \mathbf{x}) \|_1.

Since f is locally calibrated, the first term is bounded pointwise by \epsilon. The second vanishes asymptotically. Denoting k \geq \max_y p(y), we obtain M(f) \leq \epsilon \cdot k. \square

G.3 General Binning Calibration

Definition G.5 (General Binning Metric). Let \beta: \Delta^C \to \{1, \ldots, m_B\} partition the probability simplex into m_B bins \{B_b\}_{b=1}^{m_B}, and let \phi: [0, 1] \times [0, 1] \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} be a scalar comparator. The general multiclass bin-based calibration error is:

\mathcal{E}(D; \phi; \beta) = \sum_{b=1}^{m_B} \sum_{c=1}^C w_{b,c} \cdot \pi_c \cdot \phi(\text{freq}_{b,c}, \text{conf}_{b,c}).

Proposition G.1. ECE is a special case of \mathcal{E} with \phi(a, b) = |a - b| and w_{b,c} = |B_b|/n.

Proof. Substituting \phi(a, b) = |a - b| and w_{b,c} = |B_b|/n:

\mathcal{E}(D; |\cdot - \cdot|; \beta) = \sum_{b=1}^{m_B} \frac{|B_b|}{n} \sum_{c=1}^C \pi_c |\text{freq}_{b,c} - \text{conf}_{b,c}|.

For binary classification, C = 2 and \pi_1 = P(Y = 1), yielding standard ECE. \square

G.4 Calibration of the Recoverability Verifier

Definition G.6 (Per-Dimension Calibration). The verifier \hat{\mathbf{R}} is calibrated dimension-wise if for each d:

P(R^{\star,\text{bin}}_{r,d} = 1 \mid \hat{r}_{r,d} = q) = q \quad \forall q \in [0, 1].

Theorem G.2 (Non-Preservation under Scalar Summary). Let \hat{R} = g(\hat{\mathbf{R}}) be a scalar summary. If \hat{\mathbf{R}} is dimension-wise calibrated, then \hat{R} is calibrated only if g preserves the calibration property.

Proof. Calibration is not generally preserved under arbitrary transformations. If g is the identity on a single dimension, calibration is preserved. If g is a weighted average, calibration may be lost. \square

Corollary G.1. The vector verifier \hat{\mathbf{R}} should be used for authority decisions, not the scalar summary \hat{R} = g(\hat{\mathbf{R}}).

Appendix H: Recoverability Functional

H.1 Definition and Properties

Definition H.1 (Recoverability Functional). The recoverability functional is:

R_r^\star = \mathcal{R}(S_f, D_f, u, B_R, \mathcal{E}) \in [0, 1]^6,

with components as defined in the main text.

Proposition H.1 (Boundedness). Each r^\star_d \in [0, 1].

Proof. Each component is a conditional probability, hence in [0, 1]. \square

Proposition H.2 (Monotonicity in Budget). If B_R^{(1)} \leq B_R^{(2)}, then r^\star_{\text{cost}}(B_R^{(1)}) \geq r^\star_{\text{cost}}(B_R^{(2)}).

Proof. The event \{\text{cost} \leq B_R^{(1)}\} \subseteq \{\text{cost} \leq B_R^{(2)}\}, so the conditional probability is larger for the smaller budget. \square

H.2 Non-Identifiability

Axiom H.1 (Non-Identifiability). R_r^\star \neq \hat{\mathbf{R}}_r in general.

Proposition H.3. The controller cannot access R_r^\star.

Proof. R_r^\star is defined as a vector of counterfactual probabilities. These are not directly observable from any finite sample of executions. The verifier \hat{\mathbf{R}}_r is a function of observable evidence and may differ due to estimation error, model misspecification, or unobserved confounders. \square

H.3 Evidence Layer

Definition H.2 (Verification Evidence). The evidence E^R_r \in \mathcal{E} is the tuple of independent artefacts supporting \hat{\mathbf{R}}_r:

E^R_r = (\text{checkpoint hashes}, \text{invariant checks}, \text{external-effect logs}, \text{sandbox rollback proofs}, \text{verifier signatures}).

Proposition H.4 (Sufficiency of Evidence). The evidence E^R_r is sufficient for verification if:

P(R^\star_{r,d} \geq \theta_d \mid E^R_r) \geq 1 - \alpha_d

for pre-registered thresholds \theta_d and significance levels \alpha_d. \square

H.4 Expected Recovery Regret

Definition H.3 (Expected Recovery Regret). The Expected Recovery Regret (ERR) of a recovery policy \pi is:

\text{ERR}(\pi) = \mathbb{E}[\text{loss}(\pi)] - \mathbb{E}[\text{loss}(\pi^*)],

where \pi^* is the optimal recovery policy under the same stochastic process.

Proposition H.5. \text{ERR}(\pi) \geq 0.

Proof. By definition, \pi^* minimizes expected loss. Therefore \mathbb{E}[\text{loss}(\pi)] \geq \mathbb{E}[\text{loss}(\pi^*)]. Subtracting gives \text{ERR}(\pi) \geq 0. \square

Appendix I: Measurement Model

I.1 The Verifier as an Observable Function

Definition I.1 (Verifier). The verifier is a measurable function:

\mathcal{V}: \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{U} \times \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{E} \to [0, 1]^6,

\mathcal{V}(S_f, u, S'_r, E_r) = \hat{\mathbf{R}}_r.

The verifier does not receive R^\star_r as input.

I.2 Measurement Error Decomposition

Definition I.2 (Measurement Error). The measurement error is:

\eta_r = \hat{\mathbf{R}}_r - R^\star_r \in [-1, 1]^6.

Proposition I.1. Under the operational verifier definition, \eta_r is a function of (S_f, u, S'_r, E_r, R^\star_r) but not of any unobservable that is causally downstream of R^\star_r.

Proof. By Definition I.1, \hat{\mathbf{R}}_r = \mathcal{V}(S_f, u, S'_r, E_r). Thus \eta_r = \mathcal{V}(S_f, u, S'_r, E_r) - R^\star_r. The error depends on the observable inputs and the latent target, but not on any variable that is causally posterior to R^\star_r. \square

I.3 Bias–Variance Decomposition

Definition I.3 (Verifier Bias and Variance). For dimension d:

\text{Bias}_d = \mathbb{E}[\hat{r}_{r,d}] - \mathbb{E}[r^\star_{r,d}],

\text{Var}_d = \mathbb{E}[(\hat{r}_{r,d} - \mathbb{E}[\hat{r}_{r,d}])^2].

Proposition I.2 (Mean Squared Error). \text{MSE}_d = \text{Bias}_d^2 + \text{Var}_d.

Proof. Standard bias–variance decomposition:

\mathbb{E}[(\hat{r}_{r,d} - r^\star_{r,d})^2] = (\mathbb{E}[\hat{r}_{r,d}] - \mathbb{E}[r^\star_{r,d}])^2 + \mathbb{E}[(\hat{r}_{r,d} - \mathbb{E}[\hat{r}_{r,d}])^2].

\square

I.4 Relationship Between R^\star and \hat{\mathbf{R}}

Proposition I.3. If the verifier is unbiased and consistent, then \hat{\mathbf{R}}_r \to R^\star_r in probability as the amount of evidence |E_r| \to \infty.

Proof. Under consistency, \hat{\mathbf{R}}_r converges in probability to its expectation. Under unbiasedness, the expectation equals R^\star_r. \square

Corollary I.1. In finite samples with bounded evidence, \hat{\mathbf{R}}_r \neq R^\star_r, and the authority policy must be robust to this estimation error.

